import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Plug-in formats, MIDI effects, and DAW support — Tanghīm</title>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />

    <!-- SEO -->
    <meta name="description" content="Why Tanghīm works differently in Logic Pro and Ableton Live: the plug-in format constraints, MIDI routing differences, and why the Ableton path uses Max for Live rather than a VST3." />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://tanghim.app/articles/cross-platform" />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-[var(--brand-navy-900)] text-[var(--brand-text)] font-[family-name:var(--font-sans)] min-h-screen flex flex-col">
    <!-- Skip to main content (keyboard users) -->
    <a href="#main-content" class="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>

    <Nav />

    <main class="flex-1 py-10 px-6 pb-20 max-[640px]:py-6 max-[640px]:px-4 max-[640px]:pb-16" id="main-content">

      <article class="article-prose max-w-[75ch] mx-auto leading-[1.75] text-[1.0625rem] max-[640px]:text-[1rem]">

        <header class="mb-12 pb-8 border-b border-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--brand-gold)_15%,transparent)]">
          <h1 class="text-[clamp(1.5rem,4vw,2.25rem)] font-bold leading-[1.2] text-[var(--brand-text)] m-0 mb-4 tracking-[-0.02em] max-[640px]:text-[1.5rem]">Plug-in formats, MIDI effects, and DAW support</h1>
          <p class="text-[0.9rem] text-[var(--brand-muted)] m-0 mb-6 tracking-[0.02em]">Khyam Allami</p>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] text-[var(--brand-text)] leading-[1.75] m-0">
            Getting Tanghīm to work the same way across different programs and operating systems was
            the most involved part of the project. The reasons are specific and worth explaining,
            because they are not obvious. This document starts with the terms, then sets out the facts.
          </p>
        </header>

        <section aria-labelledby="h-terms">
          <h2 id="h-terms">The terms</h2>

          <p>
            A <strong>DAW</strong> (digital audio workstation) is the program a musician records and arranges in —
            Ableton Live, Apple Logic Pro, Reaper, and so on. A <strong>plug-in</strong> is a smaller piece of
            software that runs <em>inside</em> a DAW: an instrument, an effect, or a tuning tool like
            Tanghīm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plug-ins come in competing <strong>formats</strong> — standards for how the plug-in and the host DAW
            talk to each other. The main ones are:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>VST</strong> (Virtual Studio Technology), created by Steinberg, in two incompatible
              generations: <strong>VST2</strong> (older) and <strong>VST3</strong> (current).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>AU</strong> (Audio Units), Apple's format, the only one Logic Pro uses.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>CLAP</strong>, a newer, open format.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            A plug-in only runs in a DAW that supports its format, so the same tool is normally built
            in several formats at once. Tanghīm is built as VST3, AU, and CLAP.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plug-ins also come in <strong>kinds</strong>, defined by what flows through them. The data that
            represents <em>notes</em> — which key is played, how hard, and so on — is called <strong>MIDI</strong>, and
            it is separate from audio (the actual sound). An <strong>instrument</strong> receives MIDI and produces
            sound. An <strong>audio effect</strong> processes sound. A <strong>MIDI effect</strong> receives MIDI and outputs
            <em>modified</em> MIDI, producing no sound of its own; it sits before an instrument and changes
            what that instrument plays.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tanghīm's retuning is a MIDI effect. It takes the notes you play and re-sends them retuned
            to the correct pitches, using <strong>pitch bend</strong> (small per-note pitch adjustments). It can do
            this through <strong>MPE</strong> (MIDI Polyphonic Expression), which spreads notes across separate MIDI
            channels so each can be bent independently, or through a simpler single-channel mode for
            ordinary synthesisers. The difficulty described below comes from one fact: a <em>MIDI effect
            that outputs MIDI</em> is supported very differently from one DAW and format to the next.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="h-formats">
          <h2 id="h-formats">Plug-in formats and MIDI</h2>

          <p>
            VST2 passed raw MIDI in and out, which made MIDI-effect plug-ins simple to build. New VST2
            development is no longer open, however. Steinberg's licensing terms state that a developer
            who signed the VST2 agreement before October 2018 may distribute VST2 plug-ins, and one
            who did not may not <a href="#ref-1" class="ref-link">[1]</a>. A project begun after that date cannot release VST2
            plug-ins.
          </p>

          <p>
            VST3, the current generation, handles MIDI differently rather than not at all. It carries
            notes as structured "events" (which can describe pitch to a fraction of a cent, useful for
            fine tuning), turns MIDI controller movements into adjustable plug-in parameters rather
            than a raw stream, and — according to its specification — lets a plug-in send MIDI back out
            to the host <a href="#ref-2" class="ref-link">[2]</a>. A VST3 MIDI effect is therefore possible in principle.
            Whether the host actually carries that MIDI on to a downstream instrument is up to the
            host, not the format.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apple's AU format includes a dedicated kind for this exact job — a "MIDI processor" type —
            described in Apple's developer materials as a plug-in that processes MIDI input and
            produces MIDI output, and does not produce audio <a href="#ref-3" class="ref-link">[3]</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="h-hosts">
          <h2 id="h-hosts">How the hosts differ: Logic Pro and Ableton Live</h2>

          <p>
            Logic Pro provides a dedicated <strong>MIDI FX</strong> slot on each instrument track, designed to hold
            a MIDI-effect plug-in whose output feeds the instrument below it <a href="#ref-4" class="ref-link">[4]</a>. Logic
            uses AU plug-ins only. Tanghīm's AU build is produced as a MIDI processor automatically by
            JUCE, the framework Tanghīm is written in, so it loads into Logic's MIDI FX slot and works
            directly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ableton Live is organised differently. Its manual defines only two roles for third-party
            plug-ins — instrument and audio effect; in Live, MIDI effects are built-in Live devices,
            and there is no slot for a third-party plug-in that takes MIDI in and feeds MIDI to a
            following instrument <a href="#ref-5" class="ref-link">[5]</a>. In practice, a VST3 built as a MIDI effect does not
            run as one in Live: Live's plug-in loader refuses a plug-in that has no audio input
            (recorded as Ableton issue <code>ABLDEV-00057</code>; JUCE adds a hidden, unused audio output
            specifically so such plug-ins will load at all) <a href="#ref-6" class="ref-link">[6]</a>. This behaviour is
            reported by plug-in developers and matches this project's own testing, rather than being
            stated in Ableton's published manual.
          </p>

          <p>
            Live can pass MIDI from one track to another, but Ableton's documentation notes that "Live
            merges all MIDI channels to one channel when being routed internally from track to track"
            <a href="#ref-7" class="ref-link">[7]</a>. Because MPE relies on keeping each note on its own channel, this routing
            path discards the per-note information Tanghīm depends on. The mechanism Live does provide
            for generating and preserving MPE is a <strong>Max for Live</strong> device — a small program built in
            Cycling '74's Max environment, which Live hosts as a native device — carrying a setting
            (<code>is_mpe</code>) that Cycling '74 documents as making the device MPE-capable, able to receive and
            generate MPE data <a href="#ref-8" class="ref-link">[8]</a>. For these reasons, the supported way to use Tanghīm's
            retuning inside Live is a Max for Live device, not a VST3 or AU plug-in.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="h-oddsound">
          <h2 id="h-oddsound">ODDSound's devices, and why Tanghīm's are built natively</h2>

          <p>
            ODDSound — the authors of MTS-ESP, the tuning system Tanghīm uses to share tuning data
            between plug-ins — distribute their own MTS-ESP MIDI Client as a Max for Live device
            <a href="#ref-9" class="ref-link">[9]</a>. That device works by running a <strong>VST2</strong> plug-in inside Max (using Max's
            <code>vst~</code> object, which can host a VST2 inside a Max patch); through that VST2, it sends MIDI
            out directly. The installed files confirm this: the bundle is <code>MTS-ESP MIDI Client.vst</code>,
            identified as <code>com.ODDSound.MTS-ESP MIDI Client.VST</code>. ODDSound ship two versions, standard
            and MPE.
          </p>

          <p>
            This route requires a VST2 licence. ODDSound, founded in 2013, hold one; this project,
            begun afterward, does not, so the same route is not open to it. Running a VST2 inside Max
            this way also has a known weakness — the hosted plug-in can be dropped when Live's audio
            buffer size changes, and recovered only by hand — which ODDSound note inside their own
            device.
          </p>

          <p>
            ODDSound also publish, free and openly licensed, a separate native Max add-on whose
            component reads MTS-ESP tuning data directly, with no plug-in involved <a href="#ref-10" class="ref-link">[10]</a>.
            Tanghīm's Max for Live devices are built on that. The note-handling a hosted plug-in would
            otherwise do — allocating MPE channels, generating the pitch-bend messages, holding the
            last note in mono mode — is rebuilt instead from Max's own built-in objects. This needs no
            VST2 licence and avoids the buffer-size weakness.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tanghīm provides two Max for Live receivers, one for MPE and one for Mono Pitch Bend. They
            are separate because, in Live, a device marked as MPE does not deliver notes on channel 1
            to an ordinary (non-MPE) synthesiser, whereas a typical mono synth expects its notes on
            channel 1. The mono path therefore needs a device that is <em>not</em> marked as MPE, and one
            device cannot be both. (This was established by testing in Live. A commonly repeated claim
            — that the MPE setting cannot be changed after a device loads — is not confirmed by any
            official source and is not the reason for the split.)
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="h-summary">
          <h2 id="h-summary">Summary</h2>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto [-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch] mb-6 rounded-[0.5rem] border border-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--brand-muted)_20%,transparent)]">
            <table class="summary-table w-full border-collapse text-[0.9375rem] min-w-[500px]">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b-2 border-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--brand-gold)_30%,transparent)]">
                  <th scope="col" class="text-left px-4 py-3 text-[0.8125rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-[var(--brand-gold)] w-[35%] bg-[var(--brand-navy-700)]"></th>
                  <th scope="col" class="text-left px-4 py-3 text-[0.8125rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-[var(--brand-gold)] bg-[var(--brand-navy-700)]">Logic Pro</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="text-left px-4 py-3 text-[0.8125rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-[var(--brand-gold)] bg-[var(--brand-navy-700)]">Ableton Live</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="border-b border-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--brand-muted)_12%,transparent)] bg-[var(--brand-navy-900)]">
                  <th scope="row" class="text-left px-4 py-[0.875rem] font-medium text-[var(--brand-muted)] align-top text-[0.9rem]">Hosts MIDI-effect plug-ins?</th>
                  <td class="px-4 py-[0.875rem] text-[var(--brand-text)] align-top leading-[1.5]">Yes — in the MIDI FX slot (AU)</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-[0.875rem] text-[var(--brand-text)] align-top leading-[1.5]">No third-party plug-in MIDI-effect slot</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--brand-muted)_12%,transparent)] bg-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--brand-navy-800)_50%,transparent)]">
                  <th scope="row" class="text-left px-4 py-[0.875rem] font-medium text-[var(--brand-muted)] align-top text-[0.9rem]">Plug-in formats hosted</th>
                  <td class="px-4 py-[0.875rem] text-[var(--brand-text)] align-top leading-[1.5]">AU only</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-[0.875rem] text-[var(--brand-text)] align-top leading-[1.5]">VST3 / AU (as instruments / audio effects)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-[var(--brand-navy-900)]">
                  <th scope="row" class="text-left px-4 py-[0.875rem] font-medium text-[var(--brand-muted)] align-top text-[0.9rem]">How Tanghīm retunes here</th>
                  <td class="px-4 py-[0.875rem] text-[var(--brand-text)] align-top leading-[1.5]">AU MIDI-effect plug-in</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-[0.875rem] text-[var(--brand-text)] align-top leading-[1.5]">Max for Live devices (MPE + Mono PB)</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The same Tanghīm source is released as VST3, AU, and CLAP, plus the two Max for Live
            devices for Ableton Live. Two things could change this in future: Ableton could add
            support for VST3 MIDI effects, and the Max for Live path relies on ODDSound continuing to
            publish their free Max component.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="h-references" class="references-section mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--brand-gold)_15%,transparent)]">
          <h2 id="h-references">References</h2>

          <ol class="references-list pl-[1.5em] m-0 text-[var(--brand-muted)] text-[0.9rem] leading-[1.65]">
            <li id="ref-1">
              Steinberg, <em>Licensing FAQ</em>, VST 3 Developer Portal.
              <a href="https://steinbergmedia.github.io/vst3_dev_portal/pages/FAQ/Licensing.html" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">steinbergmedia.github.io/vst3_dev_portal/pages/FAQ/Licensing.html</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-2">
              Steinberg, <em>About MIDI in VST 3</em>, VST 3 Developer Portal.
              <a href="https://steinbergmedia.github.io/vst3_dev_portal/pages/Technical+Documentation/About+MIDI/Index.html" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">steinbergmedia.github.io/vst3_dev_portal/pages/Technical+Documentation/About+MIDI/Index.html</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-3">
              Apple, <em>Audio Unit Types</em> (<code>kAudioUnitType_MIDIProcessor</code>), Audio Toolbox developer documentation.
              <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/audiotoolbox/audio_unit_types" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">developer.apple.com/documentation/audiotoolbox/audio_unit_types</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-4">
              Apple, <em>Use MIDI plug-ins in Logic Pro for Mac</em>, Logic Pro User Guide.
              <a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/logicpro/use-midi-plug-ins-lgcef1c11e8f/mac" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">support.apple.com/guide/logicpro/use-midi-plug-ins-lgcef1c11e8f/mac</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-5">
              Ableton, <em>Working with Instruments and Effects</em>, Live Reference Manual.
              <a href="https://www.ableton.com/en/manual/working-with-instruments-and-effects/" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ableton.com/en/manual/working-with-instruments-and-effects/</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-6">
              <em>VST3 MIDI plugins won't load in Ableton Live</em> (Ableton issue <code>ABLDEV-00057</code>; JUCE workaround), JUCE Forum.
              <a href="https://forum.juce.com/t/vst3-midi-plugins-wont-load-in-ableton-live/36323" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">forum.juce.com/t/vst3-midi-plugins-wont-load-in-ableton-live/36323</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-7">
              Ableton, <em>Accessing the MIDI output of a VST plug-in</em>, Ableton Help Center.
              <a href="https://help.ableton.com/hc/en-us/articles/209070189-Accessing-the-MIDI-output-of-a-VST-plug-in" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">help.ableton.com/hc/en-us/articles/209070189-Accessing-the-MIDI-output-of-a-VST-plug-in</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-8">
              Cycling '74, <em>patcher</em> (the <code>is_mpe</code> attribute), Max Reference.
              <a href="https://docs.cycling74.com/reference/patcher/" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">docs.cycling74.com/reference/patcher/</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-9">
              ODDSound, <em>MTS-ESP</em>.
              <a href="https://oddsound.com/" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">oddsound.com</a>
            </li>
            <li id="ref-10">
              ODDSound, <em>MTS-ESP Max Package</em> (0BSD-licensed Max externals).
              <a href="https://github.com/ODDSound/MTS-ESP-Max-Package" class="ref-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/ODDSound/MTS-ESP-Max-Package</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

      </article>
    </main>

    <Footer />

  </body>
</html>

<style>
  /* ── Prose descendant rules (cannot be utilities — targeting hand-written content) ── */

  .article-prose section {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .article-prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--brand-gold);
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    padding-bottom: 0.375rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-gold) 20%, transparent);
  }

  .article-prose p {
    margin: 0 0 1.25em;
    color: var(--brand-text);
  }

  .article-prose p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .article-prose ul {
    margin: 0 0 1.25em;
    padding-left: 1.5em;
    color: var(--brand-text);
  }

  .article-prose li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5em;
  }

  .article-prose strong {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--brand-text);
  }

  .article-prose em {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* Inline code */
  .article-prose code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', 'Cascadia Mono', 'Consolas', monospace;
    font-size: 0.875em;
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-navy-700) 80%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-muted) 20%, transparent);
    border-radius: 0.25em;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    color: var(--brand-text);
  }

  /* In-text reference links */
  .ref-link {
    color: var(--brand-gold);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-size: 0.8em;
    font-weight: 600;
    vertical-align: super;
    line-height: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.15s ease;
  }

  .ref-link:hover {
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  .ref-link:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--brand-gold);
    outline-offset: 2px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* References list items */
  .references-list li {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    padding-left: 0.25em;
  }

  .references-list li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .references-list em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--brand-text);
  }

  /* Reference URLs */
  .ref-url {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--brand-gold);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-gold) 40%, transparent);
    word-break: break-all;
    transition: color 0.15s ease;
    font-size: 0.875em;
  }

  .ref-url:hover {
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-gold) 80%, white);
    text-decoration-color: var(--brand-gold);
  }

  .ref-url:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--brand-gold);
    outline-offset: 2px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
